[TEMPLATE]
# Client configuration — copy to `.env.local` and fill in.
#
# READ THIS FIRST.
#
# Every value here is inlined into the JavaScript bundle by Vite and therefore
# shipped inside the APK, where anyone can read it. So a value only belongs here
# if it is safe to publish on a billboard.
#
#   · Safe: the Supabase URL, the Supabase anon (publishable) key, the PostHog
#     project key, and the URLs of our own Edge Functions.
#   · NOT safe, ever: the Supabase service_role key, the OpenAI key, the SMS
#     provider's tokens, the FCM service account, AMIS credentials, the dealer
#     system's token or connection string.
#
# Those belong to the server. They go in the Supabase dashboard under
# Edge Functions → Secrets, and the functions read them with Deno.env.get().
# A key in this file is a key published on the internet.
#
# After changing anything here, rebuild: Vite bakes the values in at build time.
#   npm run build && npm run apk

# ── Backend ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These two are the DEV backend, and only the dev backend. Staging and
# production do not read them: their project is chosen from `VITE_APP_ENV` by
# src/services/appEnvironments.ts, so a staging build made on this machine still
# reads staging even though this file points at localhost.
#
# Locally that is the Supabase stack in Docker (`npm run stack:start`), which
# prints the URL and key for this file — the API is on http://127.0.0.1:54421,
# not the default 54321, because another project on this machine uses those ports.
# Leaving them empty gives the on-device repository: the app still works, it just
# has no shared database behind it.
#
# The anon key is designed to be public; row level security is what protects the
# data. The service-role and `sb_secret_…` keys are never in this file.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ── Marketplace (the Shop tab) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# A separate production project that publishes what shops have listed: shops as
# `branches`, products as `listings`, read-only and anonymously. Setting BOTH of
# these switches the Shop tab to it, and it takes priority over the app own
# dealer projection because it is where the shops actually publish. Leave them
# empty and the Shop tab falls back to the backend, then to the fixture.
#
# The key is the marketplace anon (publishable) key. It grants reads of the
# listing view and nothing else — never a service_role key here, and never
# commit the filled-in `.env.local`.
VITE_MARKETPLACE_URL=
VITE_MARKETPLACE_KEY=
#
# Normally both are left empty. The marketplace is a different project per
# environment, and `VITE_APP_ENV` already says which environment a build is for:
# src/services/shopEnvironments.ts maps dev/staging to the AgriShop staging
# project and production to the AgriShop production project. Setting these
# overrides that mapping — right for a one-off look at another project's shops,
# wrong for a build that will be promoted.

# ── The shop app's own backend (Chat with your shop) ───────────────────────
# The Farmer App is a client of the AgriShop app's Supabase project: the
# conversation, credit asks, visits, offers and this phone's push registration
# all travel through its `farmer_api_*` calls (see src/shop/api/client.ts). It is
# a third project, with its own publishable anon key, and the farmer's own
# credential — a token the shop issues — travels as a call parameter rather than
# as a header.
#
# Like the marketplace, these are normally left empty and resolved from
# `VITE_APP_ENV`: dev and staging read the AgriShop staging project, production
# reads the AgriShop production project. Set them only to point one build at a
# different shop project.
VITE_SHOP_API_URL=
VITE_SHOP_API_KEY=


# ── Weather ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# auto (default) | supabase | open-meteo | development
# `open-meteo` calls the vendor directly from the app. It is keyless, so it is
# how live weather arrives before the backend exists. `auto` reads the
# normalised table when a backend is configured, and the fixture otherwise —
# an unconfigured build never makes a network call.
VITE_WEATHER_PROVIDER=open-meteo

# ── Our own Edge Function endpoints ────────────────────────────────────────
# These are our server, not a vendor: the key stays inside the function.
# The app appends its route (`/ask`, `/request`) to the function URL.
# AI gateway (scan diagnosis + the assistant).
VITE_AI_ENDPOINT=
# Verification: send and check a one-time code.
VITE_OTP_ENDPOINT=
# Weather ingestion: fills `weather_readings` so the UI and the AI read the same
# reading instead of making two independent vendor calls.
VITE_WEATHER_INGEST_ENDPOINT=
# Dealer projection, when the app reads it through our own endpoint instead of
# the Supabase RPCs.
VITE_DEALER_ENDPOINT=


# ── The Urdu voice (server-side only) ──────────────────────────────────────
# The app speaks the weather advice in Urdu with Gemini TTS. The key is NOT a
# VITE_ variable: everything named VITE_ is inlined into the bundle and shipped
# inside the APK, where anyone can read it. The browser posts the sentence to
# /api/tts (api/tts.ts) and that function holds this key.
#
# Set it in the deployment environment (Vercel: Project, Settings, Environment
# Variables) for Preview, Staging and Production. It is never committed and
# never printed.
#
# GEMINI_TTS_MODEL and GEMINI_TTS_VOICE are optional overrides; the defaults are
# gemini-3.8-flash-tts and Kore.
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_TTS_MODEL=
GEMINI_TTS_VOICE=

# Where the voice endpoint lives. Same origin by default (the function above);
# set it only when the endpoint is hosted somewhere else.
VITE_VOICE_ENDPOINT=

# ── Live updates (installed Android builds) ────────────────────────────────
# The channel an installed APK watches for a newer web bundle. The build bakes it
# in, and `src/services/liveUpdate.ts` polls `<base>/latest.json` on launch. Each
# environment has its own GitHub Release, so the three installs never cross over:
#
#   dev        https://github.com/abakhsh56/Farmer-App/releases/download/ota-dev
#   staging    https://github.com/abakhsh56/Farmer-App/releases/download/ota-staging
#   production https://github.com/abakhsh56/Farmer-App/releases/download/ota-production
#
# Leave it empty for a browser build and for a local debug APK: no channel means
# no update check, and the bundle inside the APK keeps running.
#
# VITE_APP_ENV names the environment the build is for, and it is also what picks
# the database (src/services/appEnvironments.ts). It is set by
# `scripts/build-apk.mjs --flavor <env>` and by `scripts/ota-publish.mjs --env <env>`;
# Vercel sets it per deployment target. `dev`, `staging` and `production` are the
# three known values; anything else resolves to no backend rather than guessing.
VITE_OTA_BASE=
VITE_APP_ENV=

# ── Analytics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PostHog project keys are write-only and public by design.
VITE_ANALYTICS_KEY=
VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com

# ── Demo sign-in (DEVELOPMENT ONLY — never set this in production) ─────────
# Two fixed identities for walking the product end to end when no code can be
# delivered. With Twilio's credentials rejected (error 20003) there is no way to
# receive an SMS, and a code that never arrives cannot produce the one thing the
# app actually needs: a real Supabase session, because every row-level policy
# matches on `auth.uid()`.
#
# This is not a bypass. It signs in with `signInWithPassword` to accounts that
# already exist, so Supabase issues a genuine token and every policy applies
# exactly as it does for a production farmer. Only the code is fixed, and only
# it is shown on screen instead of being sent.
#
# Read behind `import.meta.env.DEV`, so it is compiled out of every `vite build`
# and a production bundle cannot select the demo provider. Keep it in
# `.env.local`, which is git-ignored — and never in a hosting provider's
# environment for a production deployment.
#
# Shape: a JSON array, or { "identities": [ ... ] }.
#   destination  must match the app's own validation — a Pakistani mobile as
#                03XXXXXXXXX, or an email address
#   code         the six digits shown on the sheet, and the account's password,
#                so the two must agree (see the account setup below)
#   channel      "phone" or "email"
#
# The accounts are created ONCE, and never by the app — the app not creating them
# is what stops "any code creates an account". Provision them from this same
# variable, so the sheet and the accounts cannot disagree:
#
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<Project Settings → API → service_role> npm run provision:demo
#
# That is the same request the dashboard makes (Authentication → Users → Add
# user), and it is idempotent: a destination that already exists keeps its id,
# its party and its data, and only has its password and confirmation reset. The
# key is an admin credential and is never committed or printed; the script says
# what to do when it is missing. It needs Node 22.18+ — it runs TypeScript
# directly, which is how it reads this very variable through the app's own parser.
#
# Both channels work: the script confirms the email *and* the phone, which is what
# password sign-in by phone requires. One caveat for a phone identity — the
# sign-in itself needs no SMS provider (nothing is sent), but the project's phone
# provider has to be enabled for Supabase to accept a phone identifier at all.
#
# Example (two identities, two codes — they must differ; either channel works, and
# one of each proves both):
# VITE_DEMO_FARMERS=[{"name":"Demo Farmer A","channel":"phone","destination":"03000000001","code":"111111"},{"name":"Demo Farmer B","channel":"email","destination":"demo.b@example.com","code":"222222"}]
VITE_DEMO_FARMERS=

